    <!-- Real Estate Coach Callout -->
    <div class="coach-callout">
      <h3>💡 Real Estate Coach's Take</h3>
      <p>
        Mold spores exist naturally everywhere in the Pacific Northwest—outdoor air carries them year-round. The EPA explicitly agrees: <strong>moisture control is the key to mold control</strong>. Simply wiping down mold with bleach won't stop it if you don't fix the underlying ventilation or humidity source. Conversely, when you fix the ventilation and dry out the space, mold stops growing and dies off naturally. Also, don't panic when you see dark spots on window sills or bath tile: 99% of black-colored spots in PNW homes are harmless surface mildew caused by trapped humidity, not dangerous toxic black mold.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Educational Body Section -->
    <section class="narrative-section">
      <h2>The Core Truth: Mold is a Ventilation &amp; Moisture Issue</h2>
      <p>
        Because Western Washington experiences long, damp winters, indoor relative humidity can easily creep above 60% if air movement is restricted. Mold cannot germinate or grow on drywall, window frames, or ceiling joists without sustained water vapor or liquid leaks.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cleaning surface mold with detergent or bleach provides only temporary cosmetic relief if warm, humid air remains trapped. Once continuous exhaust fans, window trickles, or attic soffit vents are restored, indoor relative humidity drops below 50%, starving mold of the moisture it needs to survive.
      </p>

      <h2>The "Black Mold" Myth vs. Common Surface Mildew</h2>
      <p>
        One of the biggest sources of panic for homebuyers is seeing dark mold spots around window sills or bathroom grout lines. 
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Black-Colored Surface Mildew (Common):</strong> Almost all dark mold in PNW homes is standard surface mildew (<em>Cladosporium</em> or <em>Alternaria</em>). It develops on cold window glass condensation or shower caulking when bathroom fan usage is insufficient. It is easily cleaned and permanently prevented by running bath fans 20 minutes after showers.</li>
        <li><strong>Toxic Black Mold (<em>Stachybotrys chartarum</em>, Rare):</strong> True toxic black mold requires saturated, paper-faced drywall that has been soaked by active plumbing or roof leaks for weeks. It is slimy, dark greenish-black, and rare in properly ventilated homes.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Demystifying PNW "Attic Rain"</h2>
      <p>
        During cold Snohomish and King County winters, homeowners often inspect their attics and find damp roof plywood or water dripping onto insulation. In 90% of cases, this isn't a roof leak—it's a classic local phenomenon called <strong>Attic Rain</strong>:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Warm, moisture-laden air from showers or cooking rises into the attic through unsealed attic hatches or flexible bathroom exhaust ducts.</li>
        <li>If the exhaust duct vents into the attic space (rather than out through a dedicated roof cap), humid air saturates the underside of cold roof sheathing.</li>
        <li>Frost forms on the plywood roof deck during freezing nights and rapidly melts on sunny winter mornings, raining down onto ceiling drywall.</li>
      </ol>

